  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "A17",
      "statement": "The paper's CAPRISE throughput (2339 vectors/s at d=768 = 428 us/vector) is consistent with a per-element Python implementation style (see implementation_style_probe; plausible reconstruction, not a measurement of the paper's code). This reproduction uses vectorised numpy noise generation (one PCG64 call for all d Gaussians) and reaches 67996.4 single-vector encryptions/s at d=768 (same measurement basis as the paper's Phase-2 number), i.e. 29.07x the paper's CAPRISE implementation. The batch EncDB throughput at d=768 (68660.3 embeddings/s) is a different (batch) measurement basis and is therefore not used for the paper comparison. Absolute numbers are implementation-style sensitive, not scheme-inherent."
    },
    {
      "id": "A17b",
      "statement": "PHE baseline: Paillier encryption is ~2-3 orders of magnitude slower than CAPRISE here (measured python-phe + GMP; numbers depend on key size 1024/2048-bit and implementation), but RemoteRAG's reported 250-300 vectors/s corresponds to an optimised C/GPU implementation on A100; cross-hardware/cross-implementation speedup ratios must be interpreted qualitatively. Within the paper's own numbers 2339/250-300 = 7.8-9.4x ('9x') is internally consistent."
    },
    {
      "id": "A17c",
      "statement": "Phase-1: the embedding step dominates. On this machine gtr-t5-base embeds 128 queries in mps 224.680 ms, cpu 747.822 ms, while CAPRISE encrypts the same batch in 2.281 ms, so the encryption overhead is mps 1.015%, cpu 0.305% (paper: 15/79.52 = 18.86%, <19%). CAPRISE is even relatively cheaper here because our implementation is faster; the paper's 15 ms encryption corresponds to a per-element Python implementation. Embedding remains the bottleneck in both. All numbers are read from this JSON's measured medians (not hardcoded)."
    }
  ],
  "runtime_s": 37.1
